      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1]\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})=\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1]\\text{d}x & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1]-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
