    "q-348718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q348718",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)={cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
